import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 623 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 624 | 2026-10-22 | **Keys and staff section 1 cut from 7 lessons to 3, with animation.** Her feedback (2026-10-22): the first lesson refused a tap on a white key ('White keys do not count here') and coloured the white keys in groups; lessons 1-2 and 3-4 were too simple and should be merged, and 5-7 can become 'tap every C, every D... on the whole keyboard'. Now: (1) **Black keys** (`kq-black`): no letters on any key; the black keys light up one at a time with the numbers 1, 2 and 1, 2, 3 in a big close-up, then the 2-3-2-3 brackets appear in turn, then low and high along four octaves; her turn: tap the side with 2 or 3 black keys (a tap anywhere in that side, white or black, counts as that group; the wrong-answer line says how many black keys that side has) and 'which sound is higher'. (2) **Two houses** (`kq-houses`): the small house (2 black keys) and the big house (3 black keys) in a close-up, the roof drops onto the black keys, then C D E / F G A B appear one by one inside the keys in her door/inside wording; then both houses together on two octaves; her turn: tap the named white key. (3) **Every key has its place** (`kq-names`): every C lit across the keyboard, then the other letters one at a time, middle C, then twelve keys counted one by one and the seven white ones named; her turn: new question type 'tap every C (D, E...) on the keyboard' (found 2 of 4...), tap middle C or the C next to it, and 'which name follows B'. Engine: `tk` animation tick, `multi` question kind, bare keyboard (`TapKeyboard`) with close-up crop and names inside the keys. Built by `patch634.py` (+ `kq.jsx`) (`t638`). Opened every lesson at 390 px in English and Korean (all, one by one, her turn, right and wrong answers, tap-every-letter): no console errors. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
